WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.182 --> 00:00:02.640
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome to
"Google Cloud Drawing Board,"

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.780
where we doodle our
way through the cloud.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.350
Today's topic, What is Dataflow?

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.690
This video is divided
into chapters.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.820
So watch the full
video or skip ahead

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.340
to any section of your choice.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.210
Data is generated in real time
from web sites, mobile apps,

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.700
IoT devices, and
other workloads.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.490
Capturing, processing,
and analyzing this data

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.140
is a priority for
all businesses.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.710
But data from these
systems is not often

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.380
in the format that's
conducive for analysis

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.440
or for effective use
by downstream systems.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.570
That's where Dataflow comes in.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:40.020
Dataflow is a serverless, fast,
and cost-effective service

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.320
that supports both streaming
and batch processing.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.780
It provides portability
with processing jobs

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.290
written using open-source
Apache Beam libraries.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.330
And it removes
operational overhead

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.240
from your data engineering
teams by automating

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.870
the infrastructure provisioning
and cluster management.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.700
How does Dataflow work?

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.930
In general, a data processing
pipeline involves three steps.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.930
You read the data from
a source, transform it,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.570
and write the data
back into a sink.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.360
The data is read from
the source into something

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.250
called a PCollection.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.640
The P stands for Parallel,
because a PCollection

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.660
is designed to be distributed
across multiple machines.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.350
Then it performs one or more
operations on the PCollection,

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.210
which are called transforms.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:30.960
Each time it runs a transform,
a new PCollection is created.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.210
That's because
PCollections are immutable.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.820
After all of the
transforms are executed,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.430
the pipeline writes
the final PCollection

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.050
to an external sink.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.610
Once you've created
your pipeline

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.060
using Apache Beam SDK in the
language of your choice, Java

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.780
or Python, you can use
Dataflow to deploy and execute

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.170
that pipeline, which is
called a Dataflow job.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.410
Dataflow then assigns the
worker virtual machines

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.780
to execute the data processing.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.790
You can customize the shapes
and size of your machines.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:03.240
And if your traffic
pattern is spiky,

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.430
Dataflow autoscaling
automatically

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.580
increases or decreases the
number of worker instances

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.770
required to run your job.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.960
Dataflow's streaming
engine separates

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.700
compute from storage and moves
parts of pipeline execution out

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.120
of the worker VMs and into
the Dataflow service backend.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.900
This improves autoscaling
and data latency.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.480
Now, how do you use Dataflow?

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.640
You can create
Dataflow jobs using

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.350
Cloud Console UI, gcloud command
online interface, or the API.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.390
You have options for
creating the job.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:40.110
You can use Dataflow templates,
write a SQL statement,

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.690
or use AI Platform Notebooks.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.910
Dataflow templates
offer a collection

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.310
of prebuilt templates
with an option

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.590
to create your own custom ones.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.810
You can then easily
share them with others

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.580
in your organization.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.030
Dataflow SQL lets you
use your SQL skills

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:01.260
to develop streaming pipelines
right from the BigQuery web UI.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.930
You can join streaming
data from Pub/Sub

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:07.250
with files in cloud storage
or tables in BigQuery,

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.830
write results into BigQuery, and
then build real-time dashboards

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.080
for visualization.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.170
You can use AI Platform
Notebooks from the Dataflow

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.570
interface itself
to build and deploy

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:22.200
data pipelines using the
latest data science and machine

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.210
learning frameworks.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:28.410
Dataflow inline monitoring lets
you directly access job metrics

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.110
to help with troubleshooting
pipelines at both

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.110
the step and the worker level.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.600
How to secure
Dataflow pipelines?

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.640
When using Dataflow,
all the data

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.520
is encrypted at
rest and in transit.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.590
To secure the data
processing environment,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.470
you can turn off public
IPs to restrict access

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.260
to internal systems,
leverage VPC service

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.220
controls that help you mitigate
the risk of data exfiltration.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.020
Additionally, you
can create a pipeline

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.600
that's protected with customer
managed encryption keys.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.350
Now, what does it
cost to use Dataflow?

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:05.040
Dataflow service usage is
billed in per-second increments

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.900
on a per-job basis, depending
on streaming or batch data.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.850
For batch data
processing, you can

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.220
utilize the flexible
resource scheduling

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.370
feature that reduces the cost
by using advanced scheduling

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.600
techniques.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.110
Each Dataflow job uses at
least one Dataflow worker,

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.590
and the price depends on
the worker configurations.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.600
Where to use Dataflow?

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.050
Dataflow is a great choice for
any batch or streaming data

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.040
that needs processing
and enrichment

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.850
for the downstream systems
such as analysis, machine

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.740
learning, or data warehouse.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.530
Some examples are stream
analytics enabling

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.530
real-time business
insights; real-time AI

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.200
enabling predictive
analytics, fraud detection,

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.950
personalization; processing
streams of log data,

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.200
unlocking health insights
for your systems;

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.540
and of course, any
other data aggregation

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.350
and analysis scenario.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.890
Want to learn more
about Dataflow?

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.200
Check out
cloud.google.com/dataflow.

